{"parentUuid":"421bffdf-1582-490f-be2d-5863f1a6f1de","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"a4301142-ad55-4a12-a4fe-231d1eb4dad2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JTAbD5ETcxtJCFeZvLh3c5","type":"tool_result","content":"**Rendering**\n- **SVG version (`index.html`):** Combines an SVG `feDisplacementMap` filter with CSS `backdrop-filter`. The displacement map is physics-based and uses a configurable index of refraction (IOR).\n- **WebGL version (`webgl.html`):** A Three.js full-screen shader that performs real-time GLSL ray refraction.\n\n**Browser support**\n- SVG: Chrome/Chromium only.\n- WebGL: Chrome, Edge, Firefox, and Safari (per the support table). The page prompts non-Chromium users to switch to WebGL.\n\n**Performance notes**\n- The page includes no benchmarks or performance measurements.\n\n**Dependencies**\n- No build step: open `index.html` directly.\n- The WebGL version uses Three.js. The page doesn't mention other libraries or a package manifest.\n\n**License**\n- MIT, © 2026 Archis Vaze.\n- Background photos in `backgrounds/` and the default Unsplash image are excluded and remain under their original terms.\n\n**Framework**\n- None. The project uses plain HTML, CSS, and JavaScript (`ui.js`, `bg-picker.js`, `index.css`).\n\n**Limitations**\n- The SVG version doesn't work outside Chromium.\n- Minimum shape size is 60 x 40px.\n- The page doesn't document other limitations, and it lists one open issue."}]},"uuid":"096fcbe6-46ff-4220-978d-7de31cc6fe69","timestamp":"2026-10-19T11:43:34.247Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":319879,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"**Rendering**\n- **SVG version (`index.html`):** Combines an SVG `feDisplacementMap` filter with CSS `backdrop-filter`. The displacement map is physics-based and uses a configurable index of refraction (IOR).\n- **WebGL version (`webgl.html`):** A Three.js full-screen shader that performs real-time GLSL ray refraction.\n\n**Browser support**\n- SVG: Chrome/Chromium only.\n- WebGL: Chrome, Edge, Firefox, and Safari (per the support table). The page prompts non-Chromium users to switch to WebGL.\n\n**Performance notes**\n- The page includes no benchmarks or performance measurements.\n\n**Dependencies**\n- No build step: open `index.html` directly.\n- The WebGL version uses Three.js. The page doesn't mention other libraries or a package manifest.\n\n**License**\n- MIT, © 2026 Archis Vaze.\n- Background photos in `backgrounds/` and the default Unsplash image are excluded and remain under their original terms.\n\n**Framework**\n- None. The project uses plain HTML, CSS, and JavaScript (`ui.js`, `bg-picker.js`, `index.css`).\n\n**Limitations**\n- The SVG version doesn't work outside Chromium.\n- Minimum shape size is 60 x 40px.\n- The page doesn't document other limitations, and it lists one open issue.","durationMs":6377,"url":"https://github.com/archisvaze/liquid-glass"},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"421bffdf-1582-490f-be2d-5863f1a6f1de","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"C:\\Users\\chris\\.t3\\worktrees\\inkling\\t3-47ff5304","sessionId":"fad76192-bf94-45bb-9f1a-d29cda113db7","version":"2.1.303","gitBranch":"t3/redesign-resurf-inspired-ui","slug":"purrfect-munching-gadget"}
{"parentUuid":"aa0eb4f7-da4e-413c-9157-1140c206784c","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"a4301142-ad55-4a12-a4fe-231d1eb4dad2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SMGJztFP1hxSyMsikuwhD1","type":"tool_result","content":"**How it renders**\n\nliquidGL takes a snapshot of the page background with its own built-in rasteriser, not html2canvas. The README says the snapshot is \"produced by its own built-in rasteriser.\" It then refracts that snapshot in a shader on a shared canvas, using WebGPU, with fallback to WebGL2, WebGL1, and finally CSS `backdrop-filter`.\n\n**Dynamic and scrolling content**\n\n- Videos are detected automatically.\n- Other animated elements must be registered with `liquidGL.registerDynamic()` so the texture updates when they change.\n- CSS animations are not supported in real time.\n- For smooth-scroll libraries, `liquidGL.syncWith()` handles render-loop sync with Lenis and Locomotive Scroll.\n- The README is inconsistent about fixed and sticky elements. The intro says sticky and fixed elements are supported, but the notes say fixed-position elements are ignored.\n\n**Performance notes**\n\n- All instances share one canvas. The README reports testing up to 30 elements without problems.\n- `resolution` trades sharpness for memory. Snapshotting a smaller element via `snapshot` also helps.\n- Very long pages can exceed GPU texture limits.\n- `shadow`, `specular`, and `tilt` add cost, especially with many instances. Rotation and scale animations are described as expensive.\n\n**Dependencies**\n\nNone at runtime. The optional `liquidGL-helper.js` is a dev-only GUI.\n\n**License**\n\nMIT, © NaughtyDuk.\n\n**Browser support**\n\nThe README lists Chrome, Safari, Firefox, and Edge as supported, on desktop and mobile. Safari may be unstable when the lens covers more than 50% of the viewport's width or height.\n\n**Limitations**\n\n- All instances on a page must share the same effective `zIndex`.\n- Images inside the target need permissive CORS headers.\n- Content with a higher z-index than the target is excluded from the lens.\n- Preloaders should be excluded with `data-liquid-ignore`.\n- Each lens exposes `destroy()`, `setTint()`, and `setDraggable()`."}]},"uuid":"6efe3223-848a-48dd-9e7e-9f24a9bab09b","timestamp":"2026-10-19T11:43:34.371Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":409158,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"**How it renders**\n\nliquidGL takes a snapshot of the page background with its own built-in rasteriser, not html2canvas. The README says the snapshot is \"produced by its own built-in rasteriser.\" It then refracts that snapshot in a shader on a shared canvas, using WebGPU, with fallback to WebGL2, WebGL1, and finally CSS `backdrop-filter`.\n\n**Dynamic and scrolling content**\n\n- Videos are detected automatically.\n- Other animated elements must be registered with `liquidGL.registerDynamic()` so the texture updates when they change.\n- CSS animations are not supported in real time.\n- For smooth-scroll libraries, `liquidGL.syncWith()` handles render-loop sync with Lenis and Locomotive Scroll.\n- The README is inconsistent about fixed and sticky elements. The intro says sticky and fixed elements are supported, but the notes say fixed-position elements are ignored.\n\n**Performance notes**\n\n- All instances share one canvas. The README reports testing up to 30 elements without problems.\n- `resolution` trades sharpness for memory. Snapshotting a smaller element via `snapshot` also helps.\n- Very long pages can exceed GPU texture limits.\n- `shadow`, `specular`, and `tilt` add cost, especially with many instances. Rotation and scale animations are described as expensive.\n\n**Dependencies**\n\nNone at runtime. The optional `liquidGL-helper.js` is a dev-only GUI.\n\n**License**\n\nMIT, © NaughtyDuk.\n\n**Browser support**\n\nThe README lists Chrome, Safari, Firefox, and Edge as supported, on desktop and mobile. Safari may be unstable when the lens covers more than 50% of the viewport's width or height.\n\n**Limitations**\n\n- All instances on a page must share the same effective `zIndex`.\n- Images inside the target need permissive CORS headers.\n- Content with a higher z-index than the target is excluded from the lens.\n- Preloaders should be excluded with `data-liquid-ignore`.\n- Each lens exposes `destroy()`, `setTint()`, and `setDraggable()`.","durationMs":7299,"url":"https://github.com/naughtyduk/liquidGL"},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"aa0eb4f7-da4e-413c-9157-1140c206784c","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"C:\\Users\\chris\\.t3\\worktrees\\inkling\\t3-47ff5304","sessionId":"fad76192-bf94-45bb-9f1a-d29cda113db7","version":"2.1.303","gitBranch":"t3/redesign-resurf-inspired-ui","slug":"purrfect-munching-gadget"}
{"parentUuid":"fd667f34-4f8b-4b79-bf15-4bd64c2382df","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"a4301142-ad55-4a12-a4fe-231d1eb4dad2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01G5uTmr3JkjfTqZLvZV3mYY","type":"tool_result","content":"**Plasma UI** is a React component library of \"liquid glass\" panels. The site describes it as \"Liquid components for React, rendered in WebGL on canvas, inspired by Apple's Liquid Glass design.\" Its `<Plasma>` panels fuse when they touch and snap to a grid.\n\n**How the glass renders:** A single WebGL canvas sits behind the page and draws every panel. Anything visible behind a panel is refracted through it. The surfaces are built from 2D signed distance fields combined with a smooth minimum, which is how neighboring panels blend together. Controls such as `refraction`, `dispersion`, `frost`, `rim`, and `shimmer` tune the optics and edge appearance.\n\n**Dependencies and framework:** React, installed with `npm install @cruxgarden/plasma-ui`. The page says it has \"Zero dependencies, except for React.\" It also ships components such as buttons, switches, sliders, tabs, and accordions.\n\n**License:** MIT.\n\n**Maturity:** The project is pre-1.0. The page says \"the API may change,\" and the core is tested. It was extracted from the Crux Garden project.\n\n**Performance:** The effect is GPU-heavy. The page recommends desktop use and says it \"does not run well on mobile.\" Each pass covers the full viewport, and `maxSurfaces` (default 16) caps the visible surface budget because higher values cost more GPU time. A `quality` setting caps the pixel ratio, and `freezeOnScroll` helps on touch devices.\n\n**Browser support:** Chrome, Edge, Firefox, and Safari 16.4+ with WebGL2. In unsupported browsers, panels fall back to a CSS frosted style, and layout, drag, and snap still work. Server rendering is supported."}]},"uuid":"75719dac-5288-4cd7-b303-c7597231650b","timestamp":"2026-10-19T11:43:36.873Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":514502,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"**Plasma UI** is a React component library of \"liquid glass\" panels. The site describes it as \"Liquid components for React, rendered in WebGL on canvas, inspired by Apple's Liquid Glass design.\" Its `<Plasma>` panels fuse when they touch and snap to a grid.\n\n**How the glass renders:** A single WebGL canvas sits behind the page and draws every panel. Anything visible behind a panel is refracted through it. The surfaces are built from 2D signed distance fields combined with a smooth minimum, which is how neighboring panels blend together. Controls such as `refraction`, `dispersion`, `frost`, `rim`, and `shimmer` tune the optics and edge appearance.\n\n**Dependencies and framework:** React, installed with `npm install @cruxgarden/plasma-ui`. The page says it has \"Zero dependencies, except for React.\" It also ships components such as buttons, switches, sliders, tabs, and accordions.\n\n**License:** MIT.\n\n**Maturity:** The project is pre-1.0. The page says \"the API may change,\" and the core is tested. It was extracted from the Crux Garden project.\n\n**Performance:** The effect is GPU-heavy. The page recommends desktop use and says it \"does not run well on mobile.\" Each pass covers the full viewport, and `maxSurfaces` (default 16) caps the visible surface budget because higher values cost more GPU time. A `quality` setting caps the pixel ratio, and `freezeOnScroll` helps on touch devices.\n\n**Browser support:** Chrome, Edge, Firefox, and Safari 16.4+ with WebGL2. In unsupported browsers, panels fall back to a CSS frosted style, and layout, drag, and snap still work. Server rendering is supported.","durationMs":7156,"url":"https://github.com/CruxGarden/plasma-ui"},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"fd667f34-4f8b-4b79-bf15-4bd64c2382df","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"C:\\Users\\chris\\.t3\\worktrees\\inkling\\t3-47ff5304","sessionId":"fad76192-bf94-45bb-9f1a-d29cda113db7","version":"2.1.303","gitBranch":"t3/redesign-resurf-inspired-ui","slug":"purrfect-munching-gadget"}
{"parentUuid":"49158cc9-41a2-4703-9b20-da5570f2f9c7","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"a4301142-ad55-4a12-a4fe-231d1eb4dad2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FJYDyJw5iP9WY2Z5aFxXGY","type":"tool_result","content":"**Platform support:** macOS only. The README says, \"This package only works on macOS. On other platforms, it provides safe no-op fallbacks.\" Windows isn't mentioned as supported, so on Windows the calls do nothing.\n\n**How it works:**\n- It uses Objective-C++ to create `NSGlassEffectView` instances.\n- The glass view is inserted behind your web content, not over it.\n- It listens for system appearance changes to keep the effect in sync.\n- The primary path uses `NSGlassEffectView`. On older systems it falls back to the public `NSVisualEffectView`.\n\n**Requirements:**\n- macOS 26 (Tahoe) or later\n- Electron 30+\n- Node.js 22+\n\n**License:** MIT, held by Meridius Labs (2025).\n\n**Limitations:**\n- Some methods (`unstable_setVariant`, `unstable_setScrim`, `unstable_setSubdued`) use private macOS APIs. The README warns \"DO NOT USE IN PROD\" and says they may change.\n- The `NSGlassEffectView` API was reverse-engineered, not officially documented.\n- Removing and updating glass views is still on the roadmap, so view management is incomplete.\n- Windows need `transparent: true`, and `vibrancy` should not be set alongside the glass effect.\n- Custom Electron versions may require `electron-rebuild`."}]},"uuid":"5790cc92-5b13-4b0e-bc1f-e17181874021","timestamp":"2026-10-19T11:43:37.114Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":390350,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"**Platform support:** macOS only. The README says, \"This package only works on macOS. On other platforms, it provides safe no-op fallbacks.\" Windows isn't mentioned as supported, so on Windows the calls do nothing.\n\n**How it works:**\n- It uses Objective-C++ to create `NSGlassEffectView` instances.\n- The glass view is inserted behind your web content, not over it.\n- It listens for system appearance changes to keep the effect in sync.\n- The primary path uses `NSGlassEffectView`. On older systems it falls back to the public `NSVisualEffectView`.\n\n**Requirements:**\n- macOS 26 (Tahoe) or later\n- Electron 30+\n- Node.js 22+\n\n**License:** MIT, held by Meridius Labs (2025).\n\n**Limitations:**\n- Some methods (`unstable_setVariant`, `unstable_setScrim`, `unstable_setSubdued`) use private macOS APIs. The README warns \"DO NOT USE IN PROD\" and says they may change.\n- The `NSGlassEffectView` API was reverse-engineered, not officially documented.\n- Removing and updating glass views is still on the roadmap, so view management is incomplete.\n- Windows need `transparent: true`, and `vibrancy` should not be set alongside the glass effect.\n- Custom Electron versions may require `electron-rebuild`.","durationMs":5797,"url":"https://github.com/Meridius-Labs/electron-liquid-glass"},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"49158cc9-41a2-4703-9b20-da5570f2f9c7","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"C:\\Users\\chris\\.t3\\worktrees\\inkling\\t3-47ff5304","sessionId":"fad76192-bf94-45bb-9f1a-d29cda113db7","version":"2.1.303","gitBranch":"t3/redesign-resurf-inspired-ui","slug":"purrfect-munching-gadget"}
{"parentUuid":"6709d6e7-4339-402f-ba90-72be5a22cc43","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"a4301142-ad55-4a12-a4fe-231d1eb4dad2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JDMB9SmVSkn18kBQCPKVff","type":"tool_result","content":"**Liquid Glass Studio** is an open-source web recreation of Apple's Liquid Glass UI, by iyinchao. The README describes it as \"The Ultimate Web Recreation of Apple's Liquid Glass UI, powered by WebGL2 & WebGPU.\"\n\n**What it is:** It's an interactive demo app more than a library. It's a React and Vite project with an online demo and real-time parameter controls built with custom Leva UI components. The page lists no published package, so I can't confirm it's meant for reuse as a library.\n\n**How it renders:** The page describes WebGL2/WebGPU dual-backend rendering, but the TODO list includes \"Render with WebGPU\" as unchecked. WebGL2 therefore appears to be the working path, and WebGPU is planned. Rendering uses:\n- SDF-defined shapes combined with a smooth merge function.\n- Multipass rendering for Gaussian blur.\n- Custom shaders for the glass effects.\n\n**Notable techniques:**\n- Refraction, Fresnel reflection, and glare with an adjustable angle.\n- Dispersion. The page uses this term rather than \"chromatic aberration,\" which is the closest match to what you asked about.\n- Blob effect, which merges shapes smoothly. This is the page's shape-merging technique.\n- Superellipse shapes, Gaussian blur masking, and anti-aliasing.\n- Spring-based shape animations.\n- Images and videos as dynamic backgrounds.\n\n**License:** MIT.\n\n**Performance notes:** The page gives no benchmarks or hardware requirements. It claims high-performance graphics and says the multipass blur is \"high-quality & performant,\" but that's unsupported by data. Setup requires Node.js (latest LTS) and pnpm.\n\n**Credits:** SDF functions and the smooth merge function come from Inigo Quilez. Sample images and videos come from Unsplash, Pexels, and Pixabay."}]},"uuid":"15e25c6c-d626-49bb-9cab-7b7b320e18ab","timestamp":"2026-10-19T11:43:37.316Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":323114,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"**Liquid Glass Studio** is an open-source web recreation of Apple's Liquid Glass UI, by iyinchao. The README describes it as \"The Ultimate Web Recreation of Apple's Liquid Glass UI, powered by WebGL2 & WebGPU.\"\n\n**What it is:** It's an interactive demo app more than a library. It's a React and Vite project with an online demo and real-time parameter controls built with custom Leva UI components. The page lists no published package, so I can't confirm it's meant for reuse as a library.\n\n**How it renders:** The page describes WebGL2/WebGPU dual-backend rendering, but the TODO list includes \"Render with WebGPU\" as unchecked. WebGL2 therefore appears to be the working path, and WebGPU is planned. Rendering uses:\n- SDF-defined shapes combined with a smooth merge function.\n- Multipass rendering for Gaussian blur.\n- Custom shaders for the glass effects.\n\n**Notable techniques:**\n- Refraction, Fresnel reflection, and glare with an adjustable angle.\n- Dispersion. The page uses this term rather than \"chromatic aberration,\" which is the closest match to what you asked about.\n- Blob effect, which merges shapes smoothly. This is the page's shape-merging technique.\n- Superellipse shapes, Gaussian blur masking, and anti-aliasing.\n- Spring-based shape animations.\n- Images and videos as dynamic backgrounds.\n\n**License:** MIT.\n\n**Performance notes:** The page gives no benchmarks or hardware requirements. It claims high-performance graphics and says the multipass blur is \"high-quality & performant,\" but that's unsupported by data. Setup requires Node.js (latest LTS) and pnpm.\n\n**Credits:** SDF functions and the smooth merge function come from Inigo Quilez. Sample images and videos come from Unsplash, Pexels, and Pixabay.","durationMs":8495,"url":"https://github.com/iyinchao/liquid-glass-studio"},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"6709d6e7-4339-402f-ba90-72be5a22cc43","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"C:\\Users\\chris\\.t3\\worktrees\\inkling\\t3-47ff5304","sessionId":"fad76192-bf94-45bb-9f1a-d29cda113db7","version":"2.1.303","gitBranch":"t3/redesign-resurf-inspired-ui","slug":"purrfect-munching-gadget"}
{"parentUuid":"d7e922ad-a0b6-4ee1-a6e4-36952162fa2e","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"a4301142-ad55-4a12-a4fe-231d1eb4dad2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01US1Bp4uE75XXqJn85nLpHN","type":"tool_result","content":"**What it is:** Fluid Glass is a public GitHub repository for \"an interactive liquid glass experience,\" with a live demo at chiuhans111.github.io/fluidglass. The README calls it \"liquid fluid glass.\"\n\n**How it renders glass:** The page names three key ingredients: a Reaction Diffusion Equation, a Navier-Stokes Equation, and a Glass Shader. It doesn't explain how these combine. The URL examples expose tunable parameters (color, bgcolor, shadow, bright, feed, kill, iteration), but the page doesn't describe what they control.\n\n**Dependencies:**\n- OGL, a WebGL framework (github.com/oframe/ogl)\n- Vite for development and builds (`npm run dev`, `npm run build`)\n- npm for installation\n- VSCode with Volar is recommended\n\n**License:** Not stated in the content shown.\n\n**Maturity:** The project is small but has some traction: 338 stars, 24 forks, 23 commits, and 2 open issues. It has no listed releases or packages.\n\n**Performance notes:** None are given. The `iteration` parameter in the example URLs likely affects simulation work, but the page doesn't say.\n\n**Browser support:** Not stated. The use of WebGL suggests a modern browser is needed, but the page doesn't list supported browsers."}]},"uuid":"9da9b489-0d7d-4c54-9c7d-ec822fc068dd","timestamp":"2026-10-19T11:43:37.656Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":273015,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"**What it is:** Fluid Glass is a public GitHub repository for \"an interactive liquid glass experience,\" with a live demo at chiuhans111.github.io/fluidglass. The README calls it \"liquid fluid glass.\"\n\n**How it renders glass:** The page names three key ingredients: a Reaction Diffusion Equation, a Navier-Stokes Equation, and a Glass Shader. It doesn't explain how these combine. The URL examples expose tunable parameters (color, bgcolor, shadow, bright, feed, kill, iteration), but the page doesn't describe what they control.\n\n**Dependencies:**\n- OGL, a WebGL framework (github.com/oframe/ogl)\n- Vite for development and builds (`npm run dev`, `npm run build`)\n- npm for installation\n- VSCode with Volar is recommended\n\n**License:** Not stated in the content shown.\n\n**Maturity:** The project is small but has some traction: 338 stars, 24 forks, 23 commits, and 2 open issues. It has no listed releases or packages.\n\n**Performance notes:** None are given. The `iteration` parameter in the example URLs likely affects simulation work, but the page doesn't say.\n\n**Browser support:** Not stated. The use of WebGL suggests a modern browser is needed, but the page doesn't list supported browsers.","durationMs":7190,"url":"https://github.com/chiuhans111/fluidglass"},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d7e922ad-a0b6-4ee1-a6e4-36952162fa2e","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"C:\\Users\\chris\\.t3\\worktrees\\inkling\\t3-47ff5304","sessionId":"fad76192-bf94-45bb-9f1a-d29cda113db7","version":"2.1.303","gitBranch":"t3/redesign-resurf-inspired-ui","slug":"purrfect-munching-gadget"}
{"parentUuid":"50c0f6c8-1f0c-45f1-969b-ab50b0ffb5e1","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"a4301142-ad55-4a12-a4fe-231d1eb4dad2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FB2i1eMb2hJoQ3irg5HpJM","type":"tool_result","content":"**What it is:** A public GitHub repository (conorluddy/LiquidGlassReference) containing one README, a comprehensive reference for Apple's iOS 26 Liquid Glass design material. It covers Swift and SwiftUI code for glass effects, containers, morphing, button styles, toolbars, tab bars, sheets, UIKit integration, known issues, and best practices.\n\n**Purpose:** The author describes it as \"a document I can point Claude at when I want to make liquid glass,\" so it works as context for AI-assisted coding.\n\n**Audience:**\n- iOS, iPadOS, macOS, watchOS, tvOS, and visionOS developers building with SwiftUI or UIKit on iOS 26 and later\n- Developers using AI coding assistants who want accurate, current Liquid Glass guidance\n\n**Web-applicable info:** Very little. The content is Apple-platform specific and doesn't cover web technologies or CSS. The closest connection is the \"glassmorphism\" topic tag on the repo, which relates to a similar translucent design style. The page itself is a GitHub web page, and the README links to external resources such as Apple's documentation and WWDC sessions, which are useful if you're studying the design system."}]},"uuid":"16b41f51-d58a-4e1c-b6d9-7c21ea15b4e3","timestamp":"2026-10-19T11:43:39.191Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":915484,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"**What it is:** A public GitHub repository (conorluddy/LiquidGlassReference) containing one README, a comprehensive reference for Apple's iOS 26 Liquid Glass design material. It covers Swift and SwiftUI code for glass effects, containers, morphing, button styles, toolbars, tab bars, sheets, UIKit integration, known issues, and best practices.\n\n**Purpose:** The author describes it as \"a document I can point Claude at when I want to make liquid glass,\" so it works as context for AI-assisted coding.\n\n**Audience:**\n- iOS, iPadOS, macOS, watchOS, tvOS, and visionOS developers building with SwiftUI or UIKit on iOS 26 and later\n- Developers using AI coding assistants who want accurate, current Liquid Glass guidance\n\n**Web-applicable info:** Very little. The content is Apple-platform specific and doesn't cover web technologies or CSS. The closest connection is the \"glassmorphism\" topic tag on the repo, which relates to a similar translucent design style. The page itself is a GitHub web page, and the README links to external resources such as Apple's documentation and WWDC sessions, which are useful if you're studying the design system.","durationMs":7026,"url":"https://github.com/conorluddy/LiquidGlassReference"},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"50c0f6c8-1f0c-45f1-969b-ab50b0ffb5e1","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"C:\\Users\\chris\\.t3\\worktrees\\inkling\\t3-47ff5304","sessionId":"fad76192-bf94-45bb-9f1a-d29cda113db7","version":"2.1.303","gitBranch":"t3/redesign-resurf-inspired-ui","slug":"purrfect-munching-gadget"}
